course: dictionary
lesson: 11030
title: Old words for journeys
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are old, and you rarely hear them today. You will meet them in old stories, songs and poems, so it helps to work them out from the words around them. Context is your best clue.
explain: «Mechanically» the Rat repacked the basket, then chose «a stout stick for his wayfaring». A stick for a journey tells you that wayfaring is about travelling. Try your meaning in the sentence: a stout stick for travelling on foot makes sense.
explain: In The Wind in the Willows, the chapter «Wayfarers All» has a wayfaring rat, the Sea Rat, who tells Water Rat about faraway ports. He almost tempts Water Rat to leave the river, and Mole has to hold him back. A wayfarer is a traveller, so the word fits a rat who roams from place to place.
fact: «Wayfaring» means travelling, especially on foot from place to place.
fact: A «wayfarer» is a traveller, someone who journeys from place to place.
fact: Wayfaring is an old, poetic word, so you will mostly find it in old stories, songs and poems.
fact: Old songs sing of «a poor wayfaring stranger», meaning a poor traveller far from home.
fact: The wayfaring tree is a real English shrub that grows by old paths.
exercise: «The Rat chose a stout stick for his wayfaring.» What is the stick for? | *Walking on a long journey | Fishing in the river | Sleeping at night | Playing a game
exercise: Wayfaring means travelling on foot from place to place. What is a person who travels in this way called? | Boatman | *Wayfarer | Miller | Gardener
exercise: In the old song «a poor wayfaring stranger», what does wayfaring tell us about the stranger? | He is a rich king | He cannot speak | *He is travelling far from home | He never leaves his house
test: The Sea Rat is a wayfaring rat who tells Water Rat about faraway ports. What does wayfaring suggest about the Sea Rat? | He stays at home by the river | He is afraid of water | He can fly | *He travels from place to place
test: «Mechanically» the Rat repacked the basket. What does mechanically mean here? | *Like a machine, without thinking | Slowly, with a smile | Loudly, shouting at everyone | Very happily, singing a song
exam: Which is the best way to check your meaning of wayfaring in «a poor wayfaring stranger»? | Skip the word and never read the song again | Count the letters in the word | Ask the stranger for his name | *Swap in traveller and see if the sentence still makes sense
